title The second helper cost what the first one did, and the two queues parted at the head
prompt Recur the autonomous loop -- Standfast on reds first, deep-read one council-rota row, then take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first and send it as its own signed round.
think Standfast first. Six REDS row numbers read as unpaired by a naive CLOSED grep -- %69, %72, %73, %110, %122, %140 -- and each turned out to be a prose citation rather than an open row: three were fixed in-round at the time they were found, three are references from later rows to folded ones. No open agent-closable red stands, so the constructive queue opens.
think Council rota row 0 (Aether - Jupiter) read: follow-our-compass, the Lindy effect, the grain and the crossing. The compass named the read order and the grain named the strand this lap cuts with -- sameness as compression, one body rather than forty-three. Lindy carried the choice of what to assert: a meter read for years earns its numbers written as measurements rather than as recollections, which is why the queue disagreement below became two asserted rows rather than a sentence.
think Lap 185 by git log --oneline --grep="^caravan:" | wc -l returning 184 before this round, so the rota reads 185 mod 5 = 0.
think choose_pass led the fold queue at 924 carried over forty-three rungs, and the reach meter priced it at 129 openings -- 86 function and 43 import. The function column was the widest of the arc, two per rung rather than one, because the body calls two private helpers, waits and may_pass. Both already stand lifted in caravan/ladder_checks.rye from earlier laps, so the lifted body reaches the pair standing in the harness and publishes nothing. The import column named system, which the harness imports at its own head.
think door_admits is the third helper the body calls and the meter never priced it at all, since it already stands public in all forty-three rungs and lifted in the harness beside the other two. That is the arc's own shape paying forward twice in one lap.
think The identical set and the folding set are the same 43 here, proven by hashing every choose_pass body with the visibility keyword stripped: none of the forty-three stands below the fold line and none keeps its Table private. Earlier families in this arc split 48 folding into 43 plus 5; this one does not split at all.
think The fall predicted 630 and measured 630 exactly -- carry 62,964 to 62,334 -- the third lap in a row where the scan's own formula named its next reading before the fold ran.
obs The copy witness caught two things the author would have shipped. First, a doc comment writing the harness parameter name into prose, refused by HARNESS_PROSE_BAD; the sentence now names the rung's lookahead ceiling in words. Second, the recited-count guard fired on a new fold-queue sentence reciting fifty-nine in words with no assert holding it, so the sentence points at the reach meter's identical set rather than at a number.
obs The finding worth carrying: the two queues parted at the head this lap and both are now asserted rather than described. The carry queue leads with mend_the_plan at 684 carried, which would fall by 72 -- its signature runs seventeen lines, so the stub is nearly as long as the body. The fall queue leads with read_the_reply at 414 -- ten rungs at forty-nine lines over a three-line stub, widening nothing of any kind. Carry is what a family costs today; fall is what a lift actually returns.
obs asked_of stands second by fall at 360 and is the only near row whose widening still names a private function -- 31 function openings beside 31 constant openings. The lift-underneath answer that carried this arc will have to reach a constant as well as a helper when that row comes up, which is a genuinely new column rather than a wider old one.
